{
  "image_name": "41893_2025_1724_Fig1_HTML.webp",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a strategy for compatibilizing mixed plastic waste with a reactive additive. At the top, it contrasts an earlier dynamic-bond approach using fluorinated diazirines with a newer siloxane-based additive carrying diazomalonate groups. On heating, the diazomalonate releases nitrogen to form a carbene, which inserts into C–H bonds on nearby polymer chains. This creates covalent links between otherwise incompatible polymers.\n\nThe lower part applies this idea to common packaging plastics, especially PE, PP, and PBAT. A small table indicates that the target polymers melt below or around the processing temperature, while PET appears unsuitable because its melting point is much higher. The mixed plastics are combined with the universal dynamic compatibilizer and processed in a melt extruder at about 180 °C, yielding an upcycled product.\n\nA schematic comparison on the right shows the key effect: without the compatibilizer, the blend phase-separates into distinct domains, with PE and PP grouped apart from polar PBAT. With the compatibilizer, the material becomes more uniform, suggesting improved interfacial adhesion and mixing. Overall, the image presents a heat-activated carbene chemistry that enables one additive to improve recycling of mixed polyolefin/polyester waste.",
